  "worked_solution": "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2})}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{F(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{L}^{-1}{\\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(\u03c4)\\,\\text{d}\u03c4}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{G(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{F(s)}{s}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(\u03c4)\\,\\text{d}\u03c4 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi \u03c4)\\,\\text{d}\u03c4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi \u03c4)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
